Why Nest.js (on Express) for new microservices
- Opinionated architecture (modules, providers, DI) on top of familiar Express runtime → faster scaffolding and consistency.
- First-class testing utilities and CLI schematics accelerate templating new services.
- Mature ecosystem: @nestjs/mongoose, validation pipes, interceptors/guards/filters for cross-cutting concerns.

In-memory XML vs MongoDB
- Feasibility: In-memory is viable for small/medium datasets with fast cold-start if XML is moderate. Lowest latency, zero external dependency.
- Implications: Memory-bound; pod reschedules trigger reload parse; lack of secondary query patterns; operationally simpler.
- MongoDB: Durable, horizontally scalable, flexible indexing; slightly higher latency; requires ops (backups, indexing, monitoring). Start with in-memory, enable Mongo when size/throughput or write needs grow.

Porting considerations from .NET/SQL
- Preserve response schemas and HTTP codes; add contract tests comparing old vs new.
- Data type fidelity (GUIDs, decimals, dates/time zones); normalization parity; watch for null vs empty.
- Replace SQL joins with embedding or referencing patterns in documents; precompute lookup indexes when needed.

Next steps
1) Lock API contract and normalization rules. 2) Implement Phase 1 service and parity tests. 3) Wire CI and containerization. 4) Stand up Mongo and ETL. 5) Canary and cut over.

Here are the main reasons your original awk one-liner can fail, followed by practical tips and best practices for using awk with GFF3.

Why the original command might not work
- Exons don’t reference genes directly: In GFF3, exon features typically have Parent pointing to transcript IDs (Parent=transcript:... or Parent=<transcript_id>), not to genes. You must map transcript → gene first, then count exons per gene.
- “Last seen gene” is unsafe: Keeping a single variable set on $3=="gene" and incrementing for any following exon line assumes all subsequent exons belong to that gene. GFF3 is not structured that way; features are interleaved.
- Attribute order and whitespace vary: The 9th column (attributes) is a semicolon-delimited list where order is not guaranteed. Tokens may contain leading spaces (" Parent=..."). Matching ^ID=... without trimming or scanning all tokens can miss values.
- Typed prefixes break direct matches: Many Ensembl-style GFF3s use typed IDs like ID=gene:..., Parent=transcript:.... If you don’t strip these prefixes consistently, transcript IDs won’t match Parent values and your map will be empty.
- Multiple parents per exon: Parent can be a comma-separated list of transcripts. If you don’t split on commas and handle each parent, you’ll undercount.
- Feature name synonyms: Some files use transcript, others mRNA. If you only match one, you miss mapping records.
- GFF3 vs GTF confusion: If the file is actually GTF, it uses gene_id and transcript_id with quotes, not ID= and Parent=. The awk logic must match the actual format.
- Possible URL encoding: Some attribute values in GFF3 can be URL-encoded. If present, naive string parsing can behave unexpectedly (less common for IDs but worth noting).

Best practices for awk on GFF3
- Always parse attributes robustly:
  - Split $9 on “;”, trim whitespace on each token.
  - Match key=... pairs with a function, don’t assume order.
  - Strip typed prefixes (e.g., gene:, transcript:) before comparing IDs.
- Respect the feature graph:
  - Build a transcript → gene map from transcript/mRNA features (using ID and Parent).
  - For exons, read Parent transcript(s), resolve to gene via the map, then increment counts.
- Handle multiple parents:
  - Split Parent on commas and process each transcript.
- Support synonyms:
  - Treat both transcript and mRNA as transcript features.
- Prefer a two-pass (order-agnostic) solution when possible:
  - Pass 1: build transcript → gene.
  - Pass 2: count exons via parents. In awk, this is done by providing the file twice (FNR==NR trick) or using gawk’s multi-file pattern.
- Skip headers and malformed rows:
  - Ignore lines starting with # and rows with fewer than 9 fields.
- Decide on counting semantics:
  - If you want “unique exons per gene” (not summed across isoforms), deduplicate by genomic coordinates (seqid, strand, start, end) per gene.
- Use gawk for portability/performance:
  - Some awk variants differ; gawk is widely available and consistent.
- Debug in small slices:
  - head -n 200 file.gff3 | grep -v '^#' | cut -f3,9
  - Confirm your file uses ID=/Parent= and which feature names appear: cut -f3 | sort | uniq -c
  - Print intermediate mappings (e.g., transcript → gene) to ensure they’re being captured.

Corrected awk command (two-pass, robust parsing, exon counts per gene)
awk -F'\t' '
BEGIN { OFS="\t" }
$0 ~ /^#/ { next }
function val(attr, key,    i, f, m) {
  n = split(attr, f, ";");
  for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    gsub(/^[ \t]+|[ \t]+$/, "", f[i]);
    if (match(f[i], "^" key "=([^;]+)", m)) return m[1];
  }
  return "";
}
function strip_type(id){ gsub(/^[^:]+:/, "", id); return id }
FNR==NR {
  if (NF < 9) next;
  if ($3=="transcript" || $3=="mRNA") {
    tid = strip_type(val($9, "ID"));
    gid = strip_type(val($9, "Parent"));
    if (tid && gid) t2g[tid] = gid;
  }
  next;
}
{
  if (NF < 9) next;
  if ($3 != "exon") next;
  parents = val($9, "Parent");
  if (parents == "") next;
  n = split(parents, p, ",");
  for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    tid = strip_type(p[i]);
    gid = t2g[tid];
    if (gid) exon_count[gid]++;
  }
}
END {
  for (g in exon_count) print g, exon_count[g];
}
' file.gff3 file.gff3

Additional tips
- Verify path and format: Ensure the file is true GFF3 (has ##gff-version 3) and the path is correct. If gzipped, use zcat file.gff3.gz | awk '...'.
- Locale and speed: For large files, set LC_ALL=C and prefer gawk. Example: LC_ALL=C gawk -F '\t' '...'.
- Sanity checks: Before running the full job, preview attributes to ensure IDs/Parents look as expected and whether typed prefixes are present.

Below is a concise walkthrough of the math behind RSA key generation, followed by improved key-generation code (MATLAB using Java BigInteger, as in Part 1), and common pitfalls to avoid.

Mathematical principles behind RSA key generation
- Primes p and q:
  - Choose two large, random primes p and q of roughly equal bit length (e.g., 1024 bits each for a 2048-bit modulus). Using roughly equal sizes makes factoring harder.
  - Ensure p ≠ q and that e is coprime with (p−1) and (q−1); this guarantees that the modular inverse of e exists in the appropriate group.
- Modulus n:
  - n = p × q is the public modulus. Its size determines the security level (e.g., 2048 bits).
- Euler’s totient and Carmichael’s function:
  - Euler’s totient: φ(n) = (p−1)(q−1).
  - Carmichael’s function: λ(n) = lcm(p−1, q−1). Using λ(n) is slightly more precise because for all m coprime to n: m^λ(n) ≡ 1 (mod n). Either φ(n) or λ(n) works if gcd(e, ·) = 1.
- Public exponent e:
  - Choose a small odd integer that’s coprime to φ(n) or λ(n). The de-facto standard is e = 65537. It gives excellent performance and avoids known weaknesses of smaller e like 3.
- Private exponent d:
  - d is the modular inverse of e modulo φ(n) (or λ(n)): d = e^(-1) mod φ(n). This means e·d ≡ 1 (mod φ(n)), ensuring correct decryption because m^(ed) ≡ m (mod n).
- CRT parameters (optimization):
  - dp = d mod (p−1), dq = d mod (q−1), qInv = q^(-1) mod p. These accelerate decryption/signing by working modulo p and q separately, then recombining via CRT.

Common pitfalls to avoid
- Weak randomness:
  - Do not use predictable or low-entropy RNG. Use a cryptographically secure RNG for prime generation and seeding.
- Skipping coprimality checks:
  - Always ensure gcd(e, p−1) = 1 and gcd(e, q−1) = 1 (equivalently gcd(e, φ(n)) = 1). Otherwise d won’t exist.
- Reusing primes:
  - Never reuse p or q across different keys. If two moduli share a prime, both are broken with gcd.
- Unbalanced or too-close primes:
  - Make p and q similar in bit length, but not too close. Extremely close primes enable Fermat-style factoring. A practical check is that |p − q| is not “tiny” (has a large bit length).
- Too-small modulus:
  - Avoid 1024-bit keys in new designs. Use at least 2048 bits; 3072/4096 for long-term margins.
- Non-standard e:
  - Avoid unusual e values; use 65537. Very small exponents (e.g., 3) have foot-guns unless every use is carefully padded and validated.
- Storing phi or primes:
  - Store private keys securely. If p, q, or φ(n) leak, the key is compromised.
- Padding and usage:
  - For encryption/signatures (later parts), never use “textbook” RSA. Use OAEP for encryption and PSS for signatures to avoid structure and padding oracle attacks.

Updated key generation code (MATLAB with Java BigInteger)
- Changes vs. the initial version:
  - Uses Carmichael’s λ(n) for computing d (still compatible with φ(n)-based designs).
  - Ensures gcd(e, p−1) = gcd(e, q−1) = 1 explicitly during prime selection.
  - Adds a “not-too-close” check for p and q to reduce the chance of very small |p−q|.
  - Keeps CRT parameters for fast decryption.

% rsa_generate_keys.m
% Generate RSA key pair using Java BigInteger (no extra toolboxes needed)
% Usage:
%   [pubKey, privKey] = rsa_generate_keys(2048);
% Returns:
%   pubKey: struct with fields n, e (java.math.BigInteger), hex/dec strings
%   privKey: struct with fields n, d, p, q, dp, dq, qInv (java.math.BigInteger), hex/dec strings

function [pubKey, privKey] = rsa_generate_keys(modulusBits)
    arguments
        modulusBits (1,1) double {mustBeInteger, mustBeGreaterThan(modulusBits, 511)} = 2048
    end

    import java.math.BigInteger
    import java.security.SecureRandom

    ONE = BigInteger.ONE;
    TWO = BigInteger.valueOf(2);
    rngJava = SecureRandom();

    % Standard public exponent
    e = BigInteger.valueOf(65537);

    % Split bits across p and q (balanced)
    pBits = floor(modulusBits/2);
    qBits = modulusBits - pBits;

    % Miller–Rabin certainty: <= 2^-128 chance of composite being labeled prime
    certainty = 128;

    % Generate primes ensuring:
    % - p != q
    % - gcd(e, p-1) = 1 and gcd(e, q-1) = 1
    % - |p - q| not "too small" (avoid Fermat factoring edge cases)
    while true
        p = BigInteger(pBits, certainty, rngJava);
        q = BigInteger(qBits, certainty, rngJava);

        if p.equals(q)
            continue;
        end

        pm1 = p.subtract(ONE);
        qm1 = q.subtract(ONE);

        if ~e.gcd(pm1).equals(ONE)
            continue;
        end
        if ~e.gcd(qm1).equals(ONE)
            continue;
        end

        % "Not-too-close" check: ensure bitlength(|p - q|) is large enough.
        % This is a heuristic; FIPS 186-5 suggests bounds. We use a simple
        % conservative check: require at least pBits - 16 bits of difference.
        diff = p.subtract(q).abs();
        if diff.bitLength() < (pBits - 16)
            continue;
        end

        break;
    end

    n = p.multiply(q);

    % Carmichael's function: lambda(n) = lcm(p-1, q-1) = (pm1 * qm1) / gcd(pm1, qm1)
    g   = pm1.gcd(qm1);
    lcm = pm1.divide(g).multiply(qm1);

    % Private exponent d = e^{-1} mod lambda(n)
    d = e.modInverse(lcm);

    % CRT parameters
    dp  = d.mod(pm1);
    dq  = d.mod(qm1);
    qInv = q.modInverse(p);

    % Pack public key
    pubKey = struct();
    pubKey.n = n;
    pubKey.e = e;
    pubKey.bits = modulusBits;
    pubKey.n_hex = char(n.toString(16));
    pubKey.e_hex = char(e.toString(16));
    pubKey.n_dec = char(n.toString(10));
    pubKey.e_dec = char(e.toString(10));

    % Pack private key
    privKey = struct();
    privKey.n    = n;
    privKey.d    = d;
    privKey.p    = p;
    privKey.q    = q;
    privKey.dp   = dp;
    privKey.dq   = dq;
    privKey.qInv = qInv;
    privKey.bits = modulusBits;

    % String representations for portability
    privKey.n_hex   = char(n.toString(16));
    privKey.d_hex   = char(d.toString(16));
    privKey.p_hex   = char(p.toString(16));
    privKey.q_hex   = char(q.toString(16));
    privKey.dp_hex  = char(dp.toString(16));
    privKey.dq_hex  = char(dq.toString(16));
    privKey.qInv_hex= char(qInv.toString(16));
    privKey.n_dec   = char(n.toString(10));
    privKey.d_dec   = char(d.toString(10));
    privKey.publicExponent = e; % for reference
end

Optional self-test (unchanged from Part 1; works the same whether you use φ(n) or λ(n))

% rsa_selftest.m
% Quick self-test to verify that keys work: decrypt(encrypt(m)) == m
% Returns logical true/false.

function ok = rsa_selftest(pubKey, privKey)
    import java.math.BigInteger
    import java.security.SecureRandom

    ONE = BigInteger.ONE;
    rngJava = SecureRandom();

    % Random test message m in [1, n-1]
    m = java.math.BigInteger(privKey.bits - 2, rngJava);
    m = m.mod(pubKey.n.subtract(ONE)).add(ONE); % ensure 1 <= m < n

    % RSA operation
    c  = m.modPow(pubKey.e, pubKey.n);
    m2 = c.modPow(privKey.d, pubKey.n);

    ok = m.equals(m2);
end

Why λ(n) vs φ(n)?
- Using λ(n) ensures that ed ≡ 1 mod λ(n) is sufficient for correctness for all messages m coprime to n, and can yield a slightly smaller d in rare cases. It is standard and compatible with CRT optimization.
- If you keep φ(n), ensure gcd(e, φ(n)) = 1. The code above explicitly enforces coprimality at the prime level (p−1 and q−1), which is efficient and robust.

This strengthens Part 1 while explaining the underlying math. When you’re ready, we can proceed to Part 2 (RSA encryption/decryption of messages with proper padding).

Below is a concise walkthrough followed by a fully self-contained, compiling Go program that implements a genetic algorithm (GA) for weight budgeting with per-asset min/max bounds and a simplex (sum-to-1) constraint. The GA maximizes expected return minus a risk penalty on variance (using a covariance matrix).

Step-by-step walkthrough:
- Representation
  - Each candidate solution (Individual) is a vector of weights (float64) whose elements lie within per-asset bounds and sum to 1.
- Fitness (objective)
  - Fitness = expected return − lambda × variance, where variance is w^T Σ w for covariance matrix Σ and risk aversion lambda.
- Selection
  - Tournament selection: sample k individuals and select the one with the highest fitness.
- Crossover
  - Uniform crossover: for each gene (weight), randomly pick from one of the two parents with 50% probability.
- Mutation
  - Add Gaussian noise (mean=0, std=mutationSigma) to genes with probability = mutationRate (per gene), then repair to respect constraints.
- Constraint handling
  - After crossover/mutation, project weights onto the bounded simplex: minW[i] ≤ w[i] ≤ maxW[i], sum_i w[i] = 1. This uses a bisection-based projection on the Lagrange multiplier.

Code (save as main.go and run with: go run main.go):

```go
// main.go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"log"
	"math"
	"math/rand"
	"sort"
	"time"
)

type Individual struct {
	Weights []float64
	Fitness float64
}

func main() {
	rand.Seed(time.Now().UnixNano())

	// Example data: 5 assets (or budget categories)
	expectedReturns := []float64{0.12, 0.10, 0.07, 0.15, 0.09}
	cov := [][]float64{
		{0.10, 0.02, 0.04, 0.00, 0.01},
		{0.02, 0.08, 0.01, 0.03, 0.00},
		{0.04, 0.01, 0.12, 0.02, 0.02},
		{0.00, 0.03, 0.02, 0.15, 0.01},
		{0.01, 0.00, 0.02, 0.01, 0.09},
	}

	n := len(expectedReturns)

	// Per-asset bounds (e.g., min 0%, max 60%)
	minW := make([]float64, n)
	maxW := make([]float64, n)
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		minW[i] = 0.0
		maxW[i] = 0.6
	}

	// GA hyperparameters
	popSize := 200
	generations := 300
	crossoverRate := 0.9
	mutationRate := 0.1
	mutationSigma := 0.05
	tournamentSize := 3
	elitism := 2

	// Risk aversion (lambda): higher penalizes variance more
	riskAversion := 0.5

	// Ensure feasibility of bounds
	sumMin := 0.0
	sumMax := 0.0
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		sumMin += minW[i]
		sumMax += maxW[i]
	}
	if sumMin > 1.0+1e-9 || sumMax < 1.0-1e-9 {
		log.Fatalf("Infeasible bounds: sum(min)=%.6f, sum(max)=%.6f; cannot sum to 1", sumMin, sumMax)
	}

	// Initialize population
	pop := make([]Individual, popSize)
	for i := 0; i < popSize; i++ {
		w := randomFeasibleWeights(n, minW, maxW)
		f := objective(w, expectedReturns, cov, riskAversion)
		pop[i] = Individual{Weights: w, Fitness: f}
	}

	best := bestIndividual(pop)
	fmt.Println("Initial best fitness:", best.Fitness)
	fmt.Println("Initial weights:", pretty(best.Weights))

	for gen := 0; gen < generations; gen++ {
		// Sort population by fitness (descending)
		pop = sortByFitness(pop)

		// Keep elites
		nextPop := make([]Individual, 0, popSize)
		for i := 0; i < elitism && i < len(pop); i++ {
			nextPop = append(nextPop, copyIndividual(pop[i]))
		}

		// Generate rest via selection, crossover, mutation
		for len(nextPop) < popSize {
			parent1 := tournamentSelect(pop, tournamentSize)
			parent2 := tournamentSelect(pop, tournamentSize)

			child1 := copyIndividual(parent1)
			child2 := copyIndividual(parent2)

			if rand.Float64() < crossoverRate {
				child1, child2 = uniformCrossover(parent1, parent2)
			}

			mutate(&child1, mutationRate, mutationSigma)
			mutate(&child2, mutationRate, mutationSigma)

			// Repair and re-evaluate
			projectToSimplexWithBounds(child1.Weights, minW, maxW)
			child1.Fitness = objective(child1.Weights, expectedReturns, cov, riskAversion)

			projectToSimplexWithBounds(child2.Weights, minW, maxW)
			child2.Fitness = objective(child2.Weights, expectedReturns, cov, riskAversion)

			nextPop = append(nextPop, child1)
			if len(nextPop) < popSize {
				nextPop = append(nextPop, child2)
			}
		}

		pop = nextPop
		currentBest := bestIndividual(pop)

		// Simple logging
		if (gen+1)%25 == 0 || gen == generations-1 {
			fmt.Printf("Gen %d | Best fitness: %.6f | Weights: %s\n",
				gen+1, currentBest.Fitness, pretty(currentBest.Weights))
		}
	}

	// Final result
	pop = sortByFitness(pop)
	best = pop[0]
	fmt.Println("\nFinal best fitness:", best.Fitness)
	fmt.Println("Final weights:", pretty(best.Weights))
	fmt.Printf("Expected return: %.6f, Variance: %.6f\n",
		dot(best.Weights, expectedReturns),
		quadForm(best.Weights, cov))
}

// Objective: maximize return - lambda * variance
func objective(w []float64, expected []float64, cov [][]float64, lambda float64) float64 {
	ret := dot(w, expected)
	variance := quadForm(w, cov)
	return ret - lambda*variance
}

// Create a random feasible weight vector summing to 1 within bounds.
func randomFeasibleWeights(n int, minW, maxW []float64) []float64 {
	w := make([]float64, n)
	sum := 0.0
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		w[i] = rand.Float64() + 1e-12
		sum += w[i]
	}
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		w[i] /= sum
	}
	projectToSimplexWithBounds(w, minW, maxW)
	return w
}

// Projects a vector w onto the simplex {y: sum y = 1, minW[i] <= y[i] <= maxW[i]}.
// Uses bisection on a Lagrange multiplier tau: y_i = clip(w_i - tau, minW[i], maxW[i]) such that sum_i y_i = 1.
func projectToSimplexWithBounds(w []float64, minW, maxW []float64) {
	n := len(w)

	// Feasibility check
	sumMin := 0.0
	sumMax := 0.0
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		sumMin += minW[i]
		sumMax += maxW[i]
	}
	if sumMin > 1.0 {
		copy(w, minW)
		return
	}
	if sumMax < 1.0 {
		copy(w, maxW)
		return
	}

	// Determine bounds for tau
	tauLow := math.Inf(1)
	tauHigh := math.Inf(-1)
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		low := w[i] - maxW[i]  // if tau <= low => y_i = maxW[i]
		high := w[i] - minW[i] // if tau >= high => y_i = minW[i]
		if low < tauLow {
			tauLow = low
		}
		if high > tauHigh {
			tauHigh = high
		}
	}

	// Bisection on tau
	for iter := 0; iter < 80; iter++ {
		tau := 0.5 * (tauLow + tauHigh)
		sum := 0.0
		for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
			v := w[i] - tau
			if v < minW[i] {
				v = minW[i]
			}
			if v > maxW[i] {
				v = maxW[i]
			}
			sum += v
		}
		if sum > 1.0 {
			// Need to reduce sum -> increase tau
			tauLow = tau
		} else {
			// Need to increase sum -> decrease tau
			tauHigh = tau
		}
	}

	tau := 0.5 * (tauLow + tauHigh)
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		v := w[i] - tau
		if v < minW[i] {
			v = minW[i]
		}
		if v > maxW[i] {
			v = maxW[i]
		}
		w[i] = v
	}

	// Optional tiny correction for numerical drift
	sum := 0.0
	freeIdx := make([]int, 0, n)
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		sum += w[i]
		if w[i] > minW[i]+1e-12 && w[i] < maxW[i]-1e-12 {
			freeIdx = append(freeIdx, i)
		}
	}
	diff := 1.0 - sum
	if math.Abs(diff) > 1e-10 && len(freeIdx) > 0 {
		step := diff / float64(len(freeIdx))
		for _, i := range freeIdx {
			w[i] += step
			if w[i] < minW[i] {
				w[i] = minW[i]
			}
			if w[i] > maxW[i] {
				w[i] = maxW[i]
			}
		}
	}
}

// Tournament selection (maximization): pick 'k' random individuals, return the best
func tournamentSelect(pop []Individual, k int) Individual {
	best := pop[rand.Intn(len(pop))]
	for i := 1; i < k; i++ {
		cand := pop[rand.Intn(len(pop))]
		if cand.Fitness > best.Fitness {
			best = cand
		}
	}
	return best
}

// Uniform crossover: for each gene, pick from parent1 or parent2 with p=0.5
func uniformCrossover(p1, p2 Individual) (Individual, Individual) {
	n := len(p1.Weights)
	c1 := Individual{Weights: make([]float64, n)}
	c2 := Individual{Weights: make([]float64, n)}
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		if rand.Intn(2) == 0 {
			c1.Weights[i] = p1.Weights[i]
			c2.Weights[i] = p2.Weights[i]
		} else {
			c1.Weights[i] = p2.Weights[i]
			c2.Weights[i] = p1.Weights[i]
		}
	}
	return c1, c2
}

// Gaussian mutation with projection-based repair
func mutate(ind *Individual, mutationRate, sigma float64) {
	for i := range ind.Weights {
		if rand.Float64() < mutationRate {
			// Box-Muller transform for Gaussian noise
			u1 := rand.Float64()
			u2 := rand.Float64()
			z := math.Sqrt(-2.0*math.Log(u1)) * math.Cos(2.0*math.Pi*u2)
			ind.Weights[i] += sigma * z
		}
	}
}

// Utility: dot product
func dot(a, b []float64) float64 {
	s := 0.0
	for i := range a {
		s += a[i] * b[i]
	}
	return s
}

// Utility: quadratic form w^T * cov * w
func quadForm(w []float64, cov [][]float64) float64 {
	s := 0.0
	for i := 0; i < len(w); i++ {
		rowSum := 0.0
		for j := 0; j < len(w); j++ {
			rowSum += cov[i][j] * w[j]
		}
		s += w[i] * rowSum
	}
	return s
}

// Pretty-print weights
func pretty(w []float64) string {
	out := make([]byte, 0, 64)
	out = append(out, '[')
	for i, x := range w {
		out = append(out, fmt.Sprintf("%.3f", x)...)
		if i != len(w)-1 {
			out = append(out, ' ', ' ')
		}
	}
	out = append(out, ']')
	return string(out)
}

// Copy individual (deep copy of weights)
func copyIndividual(ind Individual) Individual {
	w := make([]float64, len(ind.Weights))
	copy(w, ind.Weights)
	return Individual{Weights: w, Fitness: ind.Fitness}
}

// Best individual in a population
func bestIndividual(pop []Individual) Individual {
	best := pop[0]
	for i := 1; i < len(pop); i++ {
		if pop[i].Fitness > best.Fitness {
			best = pop[i]
		}
	}
	return best
}

// Sort population by fitness descending
func sortByFitness(pop []Individual) []Individual {
	sorted := make([]Individual, len(pop))
	copy(sorted, pop)
	sort.Slice(sorted, func(i, j int) bool { return sorted[i].Fitness > sorted[j].Fitness })
	return sorted
}
```

Below is a minimal, single-binary Rust init named "sysx" for Devuan that: mounts /proc, /sys, and /dev; runs fsck and mounts filesystems; brings up networking; and spawns a login getty — without systemd/sysvinit, dbus, X (x11/xorg/xinit), elogind, systemctl, or any server daemons. It also includes a simple CLI to list/start/stop tasks and a tiny supervisor with restart policy and logging.

Notes
- Build with: cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu (or your target)
- Install as /sbin/init (PID 1) or test in a VM/container with kernel cmdline: init=/sbin/sysx
- Requires basic userland tools available: /sbin/fsck, /sbin/mount, /sbin/ifconfig or ip, /sbin/getty or agetty, /bin/login, /sbin/mdev or udevd if desired (not started here). No systemd/sysvinit/dbus/etc.
- Kernel should have devtmpfs; we mount devtmpfs at /dev.

Cargo.toml (example)
[package]
name = "sysx"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
nix = "0.28"
thiserror = "1"
serde = { version = "1", features = ["derive"] }
serde_json = "1"

main.rs
use nix::unistd::{fork, ForkResult, execvp, setsid};
use nix::sys::wait::{waitpid, WaitStatus, WaitPidFlag};
use nix::mount::{mount, MsFlags};
use nix::errno::Errno;
use std::ffi::CString;
use std::io::{self, Write};
use std::os::unix::io::AsRawFd;
use std::path::Path;
use std::process::{Command, Stdio};
use std::collections::HashMap;
use serde::{Serialize, Deserialize};
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
use std::thread;

#[derive(Clone, Debug, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct TaskSpec {
    pub name: String,
    pub cmd: Vec<String>,
    pub restart: RestartPolicy,
    pub oneshot: bool,
}

#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug, Serialize, Deserialize, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum RestartPolicy { Never, OnFailure, Always }

#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
struct TaskState {
    spec: TaskSpec,
    last_start: Option<Instant>,
    restarts: usize,
    pid: Option<i32>,
}

struct Supervisor {
    tasks: HashMap<String, TaskState>,
    logs_dir: String,
}

impl Supervisor {
    fn new(logs_dir: &str) -> io::Result<Self> {
        std::fs::create_dir_all(logs_dir)?;
        Ok(Self { tasks: HashMap::new(), logs_dir: logs_dir.to_string() })
    }

    fn add_task(&mut self, spec: TaskSpec) {
        self.tasks.insert(spec.name.clone(), TaskState { spec, last_start: None, restarts: 0, pid: None });
    }

    fn list(&self) {
        for (name, st) in &self.tasks {
            println!("{}\tpid={:?}\trestarts={}", name, st.pid, st.restarts);
        }
    }

    fn start(&mut self, name: &str) -> io::Result<()> {
        let state = self.tasks.get_mut(name).ok_or_else(|| io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::NotFound, "task not found"))?;
        if state.pid.is_some() { return Ok(()); }
        let log_path = format!("{}/{}.log", self.logs_dir, name);
        let cmd = state.spec.cmd.clone();
        let oneshot = state.spec.oneshot;
        let rp = state.spec.restart;
        let name_s = name.to_string();

        match unsafe { fork() } {
            Ok(ForkResult::Child) => {
                let _ = setsid();
                let log = std::fs::OpenOptions::new().create(true).append(true).open(&log_path).unwrap();
                let _ = nix::unistd::dup2(log.as_raw_fd(), libc::STDOUT_FILENO);
                let _ = nix::unistd::dup2(log.as_raw_fd(), libc::STDERR_FILENO);

                if cmd.is_empty() { std::process::exit(127); }
                let c0 = CString::new(cmd[0].as_str()).unwrap();
                let cargs: Vec<CString> = cmd.iter().map(|s| CString::new(s.as_str()).unwrap()).collect();
                if let Err(_) = execvp(&c0, &cargs) { std::process::exit(127); }
                unreachable!();
            }
            Ok(ForkResult::Parent { child }) => {
                let pid = child.as_raw();
                state.pid = Some(pid);
                state.last_start = Some(Instant::now());
                let tx_name = name_s.clone();
                let tx_rp = rp;
                let tx_oneshot = oneshot;
                let tx_logs = self.logs_dir.clone();
                let tx_cmd = cmd.clone();
                thread::spawn(move || {
                    loop {
                        match waitpid(nix::unistd::Pid::from_raw(pid), None) {
                            Ok(WaitStatus::Exited(_, status)) => {
                                eprintln!("[sysx] {} exited with status {}", tx_name, status);
                                if tx_oneshot || tx_rp == RestartPolicy::Never { break; }
                                if tx_rp == RestartPolicy::OnFailure && status == 0 { break; }
                            }
                            Ok(WaitStatus::Signaled(_, _, _)) => {
                                eprintln!("[sysx] {} terminated by signal", tx_name);
                                if tx_oneshot || tx_rp == RestartPolicy::Never { break; }
                            }
                            Ok(_) => continue,
                            Err(_) => break,
                        }
                        thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(1));
                        let log_path = format!("{}/{}.log", tx_logs, tx_name);
                        let _ = std::fs::OpenOptions::new().create(true).append(true).open(&log_path);
                        if tx_cmd.is_empty() { break; }
                        unsafe {
                            match fork() {
                                Ok(ForkResult::Child) => {
                                    let _ = setsid();
                                    let log = std::fs::OpenOptions::new().create(true).append(true).open(&log_path).unwrap();
                                    let _ = nix::unistd::dup2(log.as_raw_fd(), libc::STDOUT_FILENO);
                                    let _ = nix::unistd::dup2(log.as_raw_fd(), libc::STDERR_FILENO);
                                    let c0 = CString::new(tx_cmd[0].as_str()).unwrap();
                                    let cargs: Vec<CString> = tx_cmd.iter().map(|s| CString::new(s.as_str()).unwrap()).collect();
                                    let _ = execvp(&c0, &cargs);
                                    std::process::exit(127);
                                }
                                Ok(ForkResult::Parent { child }) => {
                                    let _ = waitpid(child, None);
                                }
                                Err(_) => break,
                            }
                        }
                    }
                });
                Ok(())
            }
            Err(e) => Err(io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::Other, format!("fork failed: {}", e))),
        }
    }

    fn stop(&mut self, name: &str) -> io::Result<()> {
        let st = self.tasks.get_mut(name).ok_or_else(|| io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::NotFound, "task not found"))?;
        if let Some(pid) = st.pid {
            let _ = nix::sys::signal::kill(nix::unistd::Pid::from_raw(pid), nix::sys::signal::Signal::TERM);
            st.pid = None;
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

fn run(cmd: &[&str]) -> io::Result<i32> {
    if cmd.is_empty() { return Ok(0); }
    let status = Command::new(cmd[0])
        .args(&cmd[1..])
        .stdin(Stdio::null())
        .stdout(Stdio::inherit())
        .stderr(Stdio::inherit())
        .status()?;
    Ok(status.code().unwrap_or(255))
}

fn mount_fs() -> io::Result<()> {
    if !Path::new("/proc").exists() { std::fs::create_dir_all("/proc")?; }
    if !Path::new("/sys").exists() { std::fs::create_dir_all("/sys")?; }
    if !Path::new("/dev").exists() { std::fs::create_dir_all("/dev")?; }

    let _ = mount(Some("proc"), "/proc", Some("proc"), MsFlags::MS_NOSUID | MsFlags::MS_NOEXEC | MsFlags::MS_NODEV, None::<&str>);
    let _ = mount(Some("sysfs"), "/sys", Some("sysfs"), MsFlags::MS_NOSUID | MsFlags::MS_NOEXEC | MsFlags::MS_NODEV, None::<&str>);
    let _ = mount(Some("devtmpfs"), "/dev", Some("devtmpfs"), MsFlags::empty(), Some("mode=0755,size=10M"));

    if !Path::new("/run").exists() { std::fs::create_dir_all("/run")?; }
    let _ = mount(Some("tmpfs"), "/run", Some("tmpfs"), MsFlags::MS_NOSUID | MsFlags::MS_NODEV, Some("mode=0755,size=10M"));
    Ok(())
}

fn fsck_and_mount_all() {
    let _ = run(&["/sbin/fsck", "-AT", "-t", "noopts=loop", "-a"]);
    let _ = run(&["/sbin/mount", "-a"]);
}

fn bring_up_network() {
    if Path::new("/sbin/ip").exists() || Path::new("/bin/ip").exists() || Path::new("/usr/bin/ip").exists() {
        let _ = run(&["/sbin/ip", "link", "set", "lo", "up"]);
        if Path::new("/sbin/dhclient").exists() || Path::new("/usr/sbin/dhclient").exists() {
            let _ = run(&["/sbin/dhclient", "-1"]);
        }
    } else if Path::new("/sbin/ifconfig").exists() || Path::new("/bin/ifconfig").exists() {
        let _ = run(&["/sbin/ifconfig", "lo", "up"]);
        if Path::new("/sbin/dhclient").exists() || Path::new("/usr/sbin/dhclient").exists() {
            let _ = run(&["/sbin/dhclient", "-1"]);
        }
    }
}

fn spawn_getty(superv: &mut Supervisor) {
    let getty = if Path::new("/sbin/agetty").exists() { "/sbin/agetty" } else { "/sbin/getty" };
    let tty = "tty1";
    let spec = TaskSpec {
        name: "getty-tty1".to_string(),
        cmd: vec![getty.to_string(), "-L".to_string(), "115200".to_string(), tty.to_string(), "linux".to_string()],
        restart: RestartPolicy::Always,
        oneshot: false,
    };
    superv.add_task(spec);
    let _ = superv.start("getty-tty1");
}

fn reap_zombies_loop() {
    thread::spawn(|| loop {
        match waitpid(nix::unistd::Pid::from_raw(-1), Some(WaitPidFlag::WNOHANG)) {
            Ok(WaitStatus::StillAlive) | Ok(WaitStatus::Exited(_, _)) | Ok(WaitStatus::Signaled(_, _, _)) | Ok(WaitStatus::Continued(_)) | Ok(WaitStatus::Stopped(_, _)) => {}
            Err(Errno::ECHILD) => {}
            Err(_) => {}
        }
        thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(500));
    });
}

fn boot_sequence(superv: &mut Supervisor) {
    let _ = mount_fs();
    fsck_and_mount_all();
    bring_up_network();
    spawn_getty(superv);
}

fn ensure_rootfs_rw() {
    let _ = Command::new("/bin/mount").args(["-o", "remount,rw", "/"]).status();
}

fn cli_usage() {
    eprintln!("sysx: simple init and task supervisor\n\nCommands:\n  sysx list\n  sysx start <task>\n  sysx stop <task>\n  sysx help\n\nWhen executed as PID 1 (init), sysx performs boot sequence and supervises tasks.");
}

fn run_cli(args: &[String], mut superv: Supervisor) -> i32 {
    define_default_tasks(&mut superv);
    match args.get(1).map(|s| s.as_str()) {
        Some("list") => { superv.list(); 0 }
        Some("start") => {
            if let Some(name) = args.get(2) { let _ = superv.start(name); 0 } else { cli_usage(); 2 }
        }
        Some("stop") => {
            if let Some(name) = args.get(2) { let _ = superv.stop(name); 0 } else { cli_usage(); 2 }
        }
        Some("help") | _ => { cli_usage(); 0 }
    }
}

fn define_default_tasks(superv: &mut Supervisor) {
    superv.add_task(TaskSpec {
        name: "fsck-all".into(),
        cmd: vec!["/sbin/fsck".into(), "-AT".into(), "-t".into(), "noopts=loop".into(), "-a".into()],
        restart: RestartPolicy::Never,
        oneshot: true,
    });
    superv.add_task(TaskSpec {
        name: "mount-all".into(),
        cmd: vec!["/sbin/mount".into(), "-a".into()],
        restart: RestartPolicy::Never,
        oneshot: true,
    });
    superv.add_task(TaskSpec {
        name: "net-up".into(),
        cmd: vec!["/sbin/ip".into(), "link".into(), "set".into(), "lo".into(), "up".into()],
        restart: RestartPolicy::Never,
        oneshot: true,
    });
}

fn main() {
    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();
    let pid = nix::unistd::getpid().as_raw();

    if pid != 1 {
        let superv = Supervisor::new("/var/log/sysx").unwrap_or_else(|_| Supervisor { tasks: HashMap::new(), logs_dir: "/tmp".into() });
        std::process::exit(run_cli(&args, superv));
    }

    let mut superv = Supervisor::new("/var/log/sysx").unwrap_or_else(|_| Supervisor { tasks: HashMap::new(), logs_dir: "/tmp".into() });

    ensure_rootfs_rw();
    define_default_tasks(&mut superv);
    reap_zombies_loop();
    boot_sequence(&mut superv);

    loop { thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(60)); }
}

How to use the sysx CLI
- List tasks: sysx list
- Start a task: sysx start net-up
- Stop a task: sysx stop getty-tty1

Behavior and supervision
- Each task logs to /var/log/sysx/<task>.log
- Restart policy: Never, OnFailure, Always; getty runs with Always
- Oneshot tasks (fsck, mount-all, net-up) run and exit; not restarted

Security and safety notes
- Rust is used for control flow; privileged syscalls and exec are via libc/nix. Care is taken to avoid panics in PID1 paths; most errors are logged and ignored to keep boot going.
- Concurrency is limited to short-lived supervisor threads and a zombie-reaper thread to keep behavior predictable in PID1.

Excluded components
- Does not use systemd, sysvinit, dbus, x11/xorg/xinit, elogind, or systemctl. It relies only on basic Unix tools present in Devuan.

Below is a complete, runnable Flask app that renders a compact /embed UI for pasting recipe text or uploading .txt/.pdf files. It validates inputs server-side (type and size), extracts ingredients, performs public lookups (Wikipedia/Wikidata/Datamuse) with timeouts and error handling, generates a whimsical “spell,” and displays clear success/error pages.

How to embed on Square:
- Deploy this app to a public URL (e.g., https://your-spell-app.example.com)
- In Square Online > Website > Edit site > Add section > Embed code, paste this exact snippet (replace the domain with yours):
  <iframe src="https://your-spell-app.example.com/embed" width="100%" height="960" style="border:0;max-width:100%"></iframe>

```python
# app.py
import io
import os
import re
import html
import random
import logging
from typing import List, Dict, Optional

from flask import Flask, request, redirect, url_for, make_response
import requests

# Optional PDF support
try:
    import PyPDF2
    HAS_PDF = True
except Exception:
    HAS_PDF = False

app = Flask(__name__)
# 2 MB upload limit (keeps widget snappy inside an iframe)
app.config["MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH"] = 2 * 1024 * 1024

logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)
logger = logging.getLogger("spell-app")

# --------- Ingredient Parsing ---------

UNITS = {
    "cup", "cups", "tsp", "teaspoon", "teaspoons", "tbsp", "tablespoon", "tablespoons",
    "g", "gram", "grams", "kg", "kilogram", "kilograms", "ml", "l", "liter", "liters",
    "oz", "ounce", "ounces", "lb", "pound", "pounds",
    "pinch", "dash", "slice", "slices", "clove", "cloves", "piece", "pieces",
    "can", "cans", "package", "packages", "pkg", "small", "medium", "large"
}
STOPWORDS = {
    "fresh", "dried", "ground", "minced", "chopped", "sliced", "grated", "crushed",
    "to", "taste", "and", "or", "optional", "about", "of"
}
SECTION_BREAKERS = {
    "ingredients", "instructions", "method", "directions", "steps", "preparation",
    "prep", "cook", "cooking", "baking", "notes", "equipment"
}
USER_AGENT = {"User-Agent": "SpellWidget/1.0 (+https://example.com)"}  # Replace with your domain
HTTP_TIMEOUT = 4.0


def normalize_ingredient_line(line: str) -> Optional[str]:
    # Remove parenthetical notes
    line = re.sub(r"\([^)]*\)", "", line)
    # Normalize and trim bullets
    line = line.strip().lstrip("-•*").strip()
    # Remove leading quantities like "1 1/2", "2", "2-3", "200 g"
    line = re.sub(r"^\s*(\d+([\/\.\-]\d+)?(\s+\d+\/\d+)?\s*)+", "", line)
    # Remove unicode fractions like "½"
    line = re.sub(r"^[¼½¾⅓⅔⅕⅖⅗⅘⅙⅚⅛⅜⅝⅞]+\s*", "", line)

    tokens = [t for t in re.split(r"[,\s]+", line) if t]
    if not tokens:
        return None

    # Skip initial unit tokens
    i = 0
    while i < len(tokens) and tokens[i].lower() in UNITS:
        i += 1
    cleaned = tokens[i:] if i < len(tokens) else []

    if not cleaned:
        return None

    # Remove stopwords
    cleaned = [t for t in cleaned if t.lower() not in STOPWORDS]

    if cleaned and cleaned[0].lower() == "of":
        cleaned = cleaned[1:]

    ing = " ".join(cleaned)
    ing = ing.strip(" ,.;:-").strip()

    pieces = ing.split()
    if len(pieces) > 5:
        # Keep likely core, e.g., "brown sugar"
        ing = " ".join(pieces[-3:])

    ing = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", ing).strip().lower()

    if not ing or len(ing) < 2:
        return None

    # Exclude section headers and non-ingredient lines
    if any(h in ing for h in SECTION_BREAKERS):
        return None
    if ing.startswith(("serves", "yield", "time", "total time", "prep time", "cook time")):
        return None
    # Avoid lines that are likely headings with non-alpha characters
    if len(ing.split()) <= 1 and not ing.isalpha():
        return None

    # Trim trailing descriptors like "to taste" or "as needed"
    ing = re.sub(r"\b(to taste|as needed)\b", "", ing).strip()
    return ing if ing else None


def extract_ingredients_from_text(text: str, limit: int = 12) -> List[str]:
    lines = [l.strip() for l in text.splitlines()]
    ingredients_block: List[str] = []
    in_ing = False
    found_section = False

    # Try to find an "Ingredients" section
    for line in lines:
        low = line.strip().lower()
        if not in_ing and re.match(r"^\s*ingredients\s*:?\s*$", low):
            in_ing = True
            found_section = True
            continue
        if in_ing:
            if (
                not line
                or any(b in low for b in SECTION_BREAKERS if b != "ingredients")
                or re.match(r"^\s*[A-Za-z ]{3,}\s*:?\s*$", low)
            ):
                # End on blank or another section header
                if ingredients_block:
                    break
                else:
                    in_ing = False
                    continue
            ingredients_block.append(line)

    candidates: List[str] = []
    if found_section and ingredients_block:
        for raw in ingredients_block:
            ing = normalize_ingredient_line(raw)
            if ing:
                candidates.append(ing)
    else:
        # Fallback: scan all lines, prefer bullets or lines with quantities
        for raw in lines:
            if not raw:
                continue
            if raw.strip().startswith(("-", "*", "•")) or re.search(r"\d", raw):
                ing = normalize_ingredient_line(raw)
                if ing:
                    candidates.append(ing)

    # Deduplicate while preserving order, limit count
    seen = set()
    deduped: List[str] = []
    for ing in candidates:
        if ing not in seen:
            seen.add(ing)
            deduped.append(ing)
        if len(deduped) >= limit:
            break
    return deduped


def extract_text_from_pdf(file_bytes: bytes) -> str:
    if not HAS_PDF:
        raise RuntimeError("PDF parsing is not available on this server.")
    try:
        reader = PyPDF2.PdfReader(io.BytesIO(file_bytes))
        pages_text = []
        for page in reader.pages:
            try:
                pages_text.append(page.extract_text() or "")
            except Exception:
                continue
        return "\n".join(pages_text).strip()
    except Exception as e:
        raise RuntimeError(f"Failed to read PDF: {e}")

# --------- External Knowledge Lookups ---------

def http_get_json(url: str, params: Optional[dict] = None, timeout: float = HTTP_TIMEOUT) -> Optional[dict]:
    try:
        r = requests.get(url, params=params, headers=USER_AGENT, timeout=timeout)
        if r.status_code == 200:
            return r.json()
        else:
            logger.warning(f"GET {url} returned {r.status_code}")
            return None
    except requests.RequestException as e:
        logger.warning(f"GET {url} failed: {e}")
        return None


def truncate(text: str, max_len: int = 300) -> str:
    if not text:
        return ""
    text = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", text).strip()
    return (text[: max_len - 1] + "…") if len(text) > max_len else text


def wiki_summary(term: str) -> Optional[str]:
    # Try REST summary first
    safe_title = term.replace(" ", "_")
    url = f"https://en.wikipedia.org/api/rest_v1/page/summary/{requests.utils.quote(safe_title)}"
    try:
        r = requests.get(url, headers=USER_AGENT, timeout=HTTP_TIMEOUT)
        if r.status_code == 200:
            data = r.json()
            extract = data.get("extract") or ""
            if extract:
                return truncate(extract, 420)
    except requests.RequestException:
        pass

    # Action API search fallback
    search_api = "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php"
    sdata = http_get_json(search_api, {
        "action": "opensearch",
        "search": term,
        "limit": 1,
        "namespace": 0,
        "format": "json",
        "redirects": "resolve",
    })
    if sdata and len(sdata) >= 4 and sdata[1]:
        title = sdata[1][0]
        edata = http_get_json(search_api, {
            "action": "query",
            "prop": "extracts",
            "exintro": 1,
            "explaintext": 1,
            "redirects": 1,
            "titles": title,
            "format": "json",
        })
        if edata:
            pages = edata.get("query", {}).get("pages", {})
            for _, p in pages.items():
                ext = p.get("extract")
                if ext:
                    return truncate(ext, 420)
    return None


def wikidata_description(term: str) -> Optional[str]:
    url = "https://www.wikidata.org/w/api.php"
    data = http_get_json(url, {
        "action": "wbsearchentities",
        "language": "en",
        "format": "json",
        "search": term,
        "limit": 1,
    })
    if data and data.get("search"):
        desc = data["search"][0].get("description")
        return truncate(desc, 160) if desc else None
    return None


def datamuse_adjectives(noun: str, maxn: int = 5) -> List[str]:
    url = "https://api.datamuse.com/words"
    try:
        r = requests.get(url, params={"rel_jjb": noun, "max": maxn}, headers=USER_AGENT, timeout=HTTP_TIMEOUT)
        if r.status_code == 200:
            words = [w.get("word") for w in r.json() if w.get("word")]
            keep = []
            seen = set()
            for w in words:
                lw = w.lower()
                if lw in seen:
                    continue
                if lw in {"good", "many", "more", "much", "little", "other"}:
                    continue
                seen.add(lw)
                keep.append(lw)
            return keep[:maxn]
    except requests.RequestException:
        pass
    return []


def datamuse_similar(noun: str, maxn: int = 5) -> List[str]:
    url = "https://api.datamuse.com/words"
    try:
        r = requests.get(url, params={"ml": noun, "max": maxn}, headers=USER_AGENT, timeout=HTTP_TIMEOUT)
        if r.status_code == 200:
            words = [w.get("word") for w in r.json() if w.get("word")]
            keep = []
            seen = set()
            for w in words:
                lw = w.lower()
                if lw in seen or " " in lw:
                    continue
                seen.add(lw)
                keep.append(lw)
            return keep[:maxn]
    except requests.RequestException:
        pass
    return []


LOOKUP_CACHE: Dict[str, Dict] = {}


def fetch_knowledge_for_ingredient(ing: str) -> Dict:
    if ing in LOOKUP_CACHE:
        return LOOKUP_CACHE[ing]

    summary = wiki_summary(ing) or ""
    desc = wikidata_description(ing) or ""
    adjs = datamuse_adjectives(ing, maxn=5)
    related = datamuse_similar(ing, maxn=5)

    data = {
        "ingredient": ing,
        "wikipedia_summary": summary,
        "wikidata_description": desc,
        "adjectives": adjs,
        "related": related,
        "sources": {
            "wikipedia": f"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/{requests.utils.quote(ing.replace(' ', '_'))}",
            "wikidata_search": f"https://www.wikidata.org/w/index.php?search={requests.utils.quote(ing)}",
            "datamuse": "https://api.datamuse.com/words"
        }
    }
    LOOKUP_CACHE[ing] = data
    return data

# --------- Spell Generator ---------

FALLBACK_ADJS = ["mystic", "luminous", "ancient", "whispering", "emberlit"]
FALLBACK_VERBS = ["stir", "summon", "invoke", "weave", "conjure"]
FALLBACK_ENDINGS = ["So mote it be.", "Thus the charm is sealed.", "Let the spell be done."]


def build_spell(ingredients: List[str], lore: Dict[str, Dict]) -> str:
    if not ingredients:
        return "Whisper into the empty cauldron; no ingredients, no incantation."

    # Collect evocative adjectives from lore
    adj_pool = []
    for ing in ingredients:
        adjs = lore.get(ing, {}).get("adjectives") or []
        adj_pool.extend(adjs)
    if not adj_pool:
        adj_pool = FALLBACK_ADJS[:]

    random.shuffle(adj_pool)
    epithets = ", ".join(adj_pool[:3]) if adj_pool else "mystic"

    lines = []
    # Opening
    lines.append(f"Hear me, {epithets} winds, as I bind this recipe's essence:")

    # Ingredient invocations
    for ing in ingredients:
        adjs = lore.get(ing, {}).get("adjectives") or FALLBACK_ADJS
        rel = lore.get(ing, {}).get("related") or []
        pick_adj = random.choice(adjs)
        maybe_rel = f", with {random.choice(rel)}" if rel else ""
        lines.append(f"- {random.choice(FALLBACK_VERBS).title()} the {pick_adj} {ing}{maybe_rel}.")

    # Add a knowledge couplet for flavor
    for ing in ingredients[:2]:
        summary = lore.get(ing, {}).get("wikipedia_summary") or lore.get(ing, {}).get("wikidata_description") or ""
        if summary:
            lines.append(f"Let it be known: {truncate(summary, 220)}")
            break

    lines.append(random.choice(FALLBACK_ENDINGS))
    return "\n".join(lines)

